**Sea surface temperature (SST)** and its anomalies, or deviations above or below the "normal" conditions, are important to monitor over time. Variations that happen for an extended period of time, or are large in magnitude, may have significant impacts on the wider ecosystem. **Marine heatwaves (MHWs)** are a topic of growing concern, as large-scale heat events can negatively affect the life cycles, food availability, and habitat of so many species that live in our oceans. MHWs are typically defined as temperatures above the upper 90th percentile of what is typical for a given area [see @Hobday2016 and @Hobday2018 for further reading about common definitions]. On the other hand, **Marine cold spells (MCSs)**, are periods where temperatures are cooler than the lower 10th percentile typically observed in a location, and may also have both positive and negative effects on ecosystems [see @Schlegel2021 for more].
:::

