## How does SST in the Northeast Pacific compare between years?

The average SST and SST anomaly of the NEP is shown below, beginning in 1981. A few years have been selected for further emphasis. 2014 and 2015, years of "the Blob", are shown in [pink]{style="color:magenta;"} and [purple]{style="color:purple;"}. 2019, another anomalously warm year occurring during an El Nino, is shown in [cyan]{style="color:cyan;"}. The most recent three years, which have also been some of the warmest on record, are shown as [orange]{style="color:orange;"} (2023), [brown]{style="color:brown;"} (2024), and [red]{style="color:red;"} (2025). 2026 progress is shown in [**black**]{style="color:black;"}.

![](./figures/NEP_progress.png){fig-align="center" width="80%"}

### Rankings of 10 warmest years since 1981

::: {.column width="45%"}
When averaging the SST and SST anomaly for each year for the entire NEP (right), the years of "the Blob" were the warmest overall since 1981. **2025** ranked at number 5, behind 2019 and 2020.

